title: Birth of a Party
tags: event
view-if: year = 1932 and month = 5
priority: 2
max-visits: 1

In the city of Calgary, there are rumblings that a new Dominion-spanning party is soon to be formed by delegates of Labour, Farmer, and Socialist organizations. 

The Left of Canadian politics has historically been fraught with division and isolation. The Federal Progressives failed miserably to mount a united front against the two old-line parties and consequently lost relevance. Out of its ashes sprung the Farmer's Parties but then still, they remain divided along Provincial lines. 

The Labour movement is doing even worse. The TLC has been noncommittal at best to the Labour and Socialist parties. And what few Leftist parties exist are divided geographically and ideologically. Without a united front, success is scarce.  

The Meeting is set to establish the basis of the party before a future convention is called to lay out a specific programme. 

Delegates from the UFC(SS) and our ILP members have been invited to the meeting. Now, we must establish what exactly we will ask for...

- @ccf_name: What name will we advocate for the new party?
- @ccf_policy: Do we seek a more radical or more reformist route for the party?

